  "reason": "The records supply several relevant pieces: R1 establishes long-duration 1400 °C powder sintering, oxygen sensitivity, and BCC formation in equiatomic HfNbTaTiZr; R11 shows BCC-to-HCP phase formation in the same alloy after thermal consolidation; R15 demonstrates additive manufacture with in-situ alloying of elemental refractory powders; R10 uses a titanium getter; and R20 establishes silicide coatings for oxidation protection on a related refractory HEA. Other records connect oxygen or oxide formation with strength and phase behavior. However, none suggests hydride-based printable ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition governed by formation enthalpies, or deliberate oxygen uptake near 1 wt.% followed by quenching to retain metastable beta-BCC. Thus the literature points toward parts of the processing, phase-control, and coating strategy, but the defining hydride-assisted extrusion and decomposition mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
